The guard is `isProjectPath(cwd, p)`, defined in `src/core/manifest.ts` (lines 42–46):

```ts
export function isProjectPath(cwd: string, p: string): boolean {
  if (typeof p !== "string" || !p || path.isAbsolute(p) || /^[a-zA-Z]:/.test(p)) return false;
  const rel = path.relative(cwd, path.resolve(cwd, p));
  return !!rel && !rel.startsWith("..") && !path.isAbsolute(rel);
}
```

It's not called at the deletion site itself — it's applied earlier, inside `readManifest()` (same file, ~lines 48–69), which filters every entry in the manifest's `files` array through `isProjectPath` before returning it. `sync()` in `src/commands/sync.ts` builds its delete list entirely from that already-filtered `readManifest(cwd)` output, so an entry like `"../secret.txt"` or `"C:\...\secret.txt"` never survives to reach the `rmSync(path.join(cwd, p))` call.

So the mechanism that stops the traversal is: `isProjectPath` rejects any path that's absolute, has a drive letter, or whose resolved form relative to `cwd` starts with `..` — and `readManifest` enforces that filter on every manifest entry before sync ever sees it.
